00:19:56,320 --> 00:20:02,639
And the right development of yoniso manasikāra is of course\h
what's going to be the next step, because acting\h\h

169
00:20:02,640 --> 00:20:08,479
out of hindrances and attending from the origin,\h
that's mutually exclusive. So by understanding\h\h

170
00:20:08,480 --> 00:20:14,079
what yoniso manasikāra is and developing that, you\h
will be gaining a clearer perspective on what the\h\h

171
00:20:14,080 --> 00:20:20,159
hindrances are and what is pressuring you. But also,\h
we've discussed that quite a few times,\h

172
00:20:20,160 --> 00:20:24,159
just to keep the precepts and not act out and\h
refine that principle that we started to talk\h\h

173
00:20:24,160 --> 00:20:29,439
about in the beginning, is already developing\h
that yoniso direction, whether you call it that or

174
00:20:29,440 --> 00:20:37,119
not. But if we now come back all the way to\h
your question in the beginning, like "What\h\h

175
00:20:37,120 --> 00:20:40,399
now determines something as wholesome\h
or what determines something as\h\h

176
00:20:40,400 --> 00:20:47,599
wholesome" well, that is fully\h
up to you discerning whether your mind\h\h

177
00:20:47,600 --> 00:20:53,039
is pressuring you and that will be an "internal"\h
criteria. And so even if the whole world says\h\h

178
00:20:53,040 --> 00:20:58,239
"Wait, what you just did there is wrong", if you\h
know that it wasn't coming out of your mind\h\h

179
00:20:58,240 --> 00:21:02,480
pressuring you, then from the point of view\h
of dhamma it will for you not have been wrong.

180
00:21:06,800 --> 00:21:09,680
That's kind of amazing.

181
00:21:12,648 --> 00:21:15,759
At the beginning it's threatening, and then it\h\h

182
00:21:15,760 --> 00:21:29,039
becomes... Well, because true freedom is\h
in the beginning very threatening.

183
00:21:29,040 --> 00:21:36,479
And that sense of independence. And they go\h
together, right? Freedom, responsibility, all\h\h

184
00:21:36,480 --> 00:21:42,079
those things, they're just different\h
facets of the same thing. So to say that you\h\h

185
00:21:42,080 --> 00:21:46,959
are fully responsible for everything you do\h
by body, speech and mind, that statement has\h\h

186
00:21:46,960 --> 00:21:51,439
got meaning only if you see that in relation\h
to your mind pressuring you. If you leave that\h\h

187
00:21:51,440 --> 00:21:56,319
whole domain out then you can say "Yes, I take\h
responsibility for what I do", but that would\h\h

188
00:21:56,320 --> 00:22:01,679
just be a hollow statement if in reality it is\h
your mind pushing you in this and that direction.\h\h

189
00:22:01,680 --> 00:22:07,359
Then it doesn't mean anything. Because it's\h
like "Oh I'm responsible, but I have... I don't have\h\h

190
00:22:07,360 --> 00:22:11,999
the choice but to react to that pressure". It's like,\h
well then you're not responsible at all. Yes, so\h\h

191
00:22:12,000 --> 00:22:19,279
then you can say "I'm responsible", but that's just a joke then. So you have\h\h

192
00:22:19,280 --> 00:22:30,239
to include that and then start training and taming\h
that mind, so that then at some point you\h\h

193
00:22:30,240 --> 00:22:36,479
will be able to start telling that mind what to\h
do, right? In the beginning it's going to be a bit\h\h

194
00:22:36,480 --> 00:22:41,359
of a struggle. You have to tame and train that\h
mind and it might not immediately listen. That\h\h

195
00:22:41,360 --> 00:22:48,799
is why you need to be patient and so on. But at\h
some point that mind begins to trust you, you can\h\h

196
00:22:48,800 --> 00:22:55,999
see "Wait, mind begins to be tamed" that relationship\h
develops and you begin to realize that the\h\h

197
00:22:56,000 --> 00:23:03,439
safety is in your mind not being able to make you\h
do things without you giving in, so it is fully up\h\h

198
00:23:03,440 --> 00:23:10,079
to you and then you realize: "Well, then the whole\h
training is to train that mind", so that it is no\h\h

199
00:23:10,080 --> 00:23:15,039
longer pressuring me in unwholesome directions,\h
but it will see for itself that it is much better\h\h

200
00:23:15,040 --> 00:23:22,399
if it calms down and then you can just live\h
happily together and your mind will be your ally\h\h

201
00:23:22,400 --> 00:23:28,799
rather than something that you need to keep an\h
eye on because if you don't, you'll end up being in\h\h

202
00:23:28,800 --> 00:23:40,079
trouble. Is that freedom from remorse? That kind of...\h
because it's an aspect of it. Not the whole thing.\h\h

203
00:23:40,080 --> 00:23:49,599
Yes, so why are you in remorse about things?\h
What does the remorse depend upon? Well, giving in\h\h

204
00:23:49,600 --> 00:23:56,719
to the pressure. And then on a later occasion\h
realizing: "Wait, that's not right." Yes, knowing\h\h

205
00:23:56,720 --> 00:24:01,119
it's not right. So there has to be some loss of\h
perspective somewhere. And then afterwards\h\h

206
00:24:01,120 --> 00:24:07,999
you sort of regain it and you say: "Well that was\h
now no good." So that is even more reason to keep\h\h

207
00:24:08,000 --> 00:24:15,519
an eye on that mind. Sort of what you what you\h
thought was a form of safety clearly wasn't a\h\h

208
00:24:15,520 --> 00:24:21,039
form of safety. Yes. So that remorse can then also\h
be a clear indicator that there's more work to be\h\h

209
00:24:21,040 --> 00:24:27,279
done. That is why it is good to keep questioning\h
and even in the suttas, for noble disciples that\h\h

210
00:24:27,280 --> 00:24:35,519
is a recurring training like, you know "Can\h
my companions find fault with my conduct?" and\h\h

211
00:24:35,520 --> 00:24:40,239
even that is then just in relation to external\h
things, to stuff that other people can see. Even\h\h

212
00:24:40,240 --> 00:24:44,639
that is still a reflection there. Then how\h
much more so in relation to your\h\h

213
00:24:44,640 --> 00:24:49,504
own thoughts that you might still be delighting\h
in and welcoming.

214
00:24:49,505 --> 00:24:52,505
That was something I've...

215
00:24:53,520 --> 00:24:59,199
been interested in because there's actually quite a lot in the suttas,\h\h

216
00:24:59,200 --> 00:25:09,101
and definitely a lot in the vinaya that\h
is clearly aiming at removing...\h\h

217
00:25:17,133 --> 00:25:25,119
a remorse that might\h
come from quite kind of arbitrary things.

218
00:25:25,120 --> 00:25:31,919
Just about how your etiquette, or how a monk should\h
walk. And there's a lot of advice\h\h

219
00:25:31,920 --> 00:25:37,839
on that, which is doesn't necessarily... if\h
you just had that you wouldn't necessarily be\h\h

220
00:25:37,840 --> 00:25:45,839
pointed in the right direction\h
of your intention. Yes, so I'm not exactly sure\h\h

221
00:25:45,840 --> 00:25:51,759
which ones you are now referring to, but sure, so\h
that there is a level of etiquette and okay, then\h\h

222
00:25:51,760 --> 00:25:56,224
you don't maybe want to be misrepresenting other\h
monks with certain behaviors,

223
00:25:56,225 --> 00:26:02,191
but you see, then fundamentally, still... It seems like it would\h
still be beneficial, because it wouldn't be there.

224
00:26:02,240 --> 00:26:14,159
So you can take that on,\h
consider it, put it into practice, but again,\h\h

225
00:26:14,160 --> 00:26:20,639
now don't just regard that as external, but\h
you can still try to see: "Could there\h\h

226
00:26:20,640 --> 00:26:28,239
be something unwholesome about those things?"
Would there be some restlessness in the mind, or\h\h

227
00:26:28,240 --> 00:26:33,919
something else that is not proper for somebody who\h
is like fully composed and self-controlled, right?\h\h

228
00:26:33,920 --> 00:26:39,599
If you walk down the street on alms round and\h
you are just window shopping, right?\h\h

229
00:26:39,600 --> 00:26:44,319
Just looking at what's on sale... if you still have interest\h\h

230
00:26:44,320 --> 00:26:49,039
in those things, you're sort of missing the point\h
in terms of your overall perspective. Or if you\h\h

231
00:26:49,040 --> 00:26:56,799
are talking loudly, or you're just laughing and\h
joking, then all of that is perhaps\h\h

232
00:26:56,800 --> 00:27:01,999
not too complicated to see that if\h
you want to train yourself on the level of which\h\h

233
00:27:02,000 --> 00:27:07,599
thoughts you are welcoming and not welcoming, but\h
then outwardly you are laughing and joking to\h\h

234
00:27:07,600 --> 00:27:13,519
the point that other people in the street actually...\h
you draw attention from those people; well,\h\h

235
00:27:13,520 --> 00:27:20,079
you cannot be behaving in that way and at the same\h
time keep an eye on what's going on mentally. So\h\h

236
00:27:20,080 --> 00:27:24,239
that's the same reason why seclusion is\h
beneficial. You don't need to talk to anyone\h\h

237
00:27:24,240 --> 00:27:30,479
and if you do not need to talk and break out into\h
speech, it will be much easier to see your mental\h\h

238
00:27:30,480 --> 00:27:36,319
chatter. It's not so easy to see your mental chatter when\h
you're talking the whole time. You don't see it.\h\h

239
00:27:36,320 --> 00:27:43,119
And in fact,  just to\h
break out into speech and say something can of\h\h

240
00:27:43,120 --> 00:27:47,679
course also come out of that same pressure from\h
the mind. So that's the same training: "Should I now\h\h

241
00:27:47,680 --> 00:27:55,039
say this or not?" And we did a whole talk on right\h
speech in the past, and it's that same\h\h

242
00:27:55,040 --> 00:28:01,039
principle. Whether something is wholesome or
unwholesome is not determined by what you say, but by\h\h

243
00:28:01,040 --> 00:28:07,279
the intention based on which you say it. And if you\h
feel pressure to say something then that is what\h\h

244
00:28:07,280 --> 00:28:15,439
makes it unwholesome, not the words that\h
you are saying. Even though when the suttas then say,\h

245
00:28:15,440 --> 00:28:20,559
"Okay, if speech\h
goes in this direction will always be wrong" just\h\h

246
00:28:20,560 --> 00:28:25,439
as breaking precepts will always be wrong, you cannot\h\h

247
00:28:25,440 --> 00:28:31,039
justify breaking those precepts and then say: "Yeah,\h
well, there wasn't any pressure." That's impossible. So\h\h

248
00:28:31,040 --> 00:28:37,599
those outer boundaries are there just to show\h
you the main principle and then it's up to\h\h

249
00:28:37,600 --> 00:28:42,319
you, now develop that same principle\h
that's now coming into sight on account of you\h\h

250
00:28:42,320 --> 00:28:48,358
being committed to those precepts by body and speech, and then\hyou just fully apply it to the mental level as well.

251
00:28:51,357 --> 00:28:59,439
And that is training.\h
And that takes also reviewing. And it's good to\h\h

252
00:28:59,440 --> 00:29:04,879
just make time for that at the end of the day and\h
consider: "Which conversation did I have? What did I\h\h

253
00:29:04,880 --> 00:29:11,919
talk about? Do I still recollect acting\h
out of pressure? And to just get in the habit of\h\h

254
00:29:11,920 --> 00:29:19,039
reflecting in that way and then over\h
time it will become easier to catch yourself\h\h

255
00:29:19,040 --> 00:29:22,879
while you are in that situation, because that is\h
fundamentally where the restraint then needs to\h\h

256
00:29:22,880 --> 00:29:32,960
happen. I think, to go back\h
to the remorse and what you say now is that,\h

257
00:29:35,948 --> 00:29:39,279
this practice of what just you\h
just mentioned... kind of recollecting what\h\h

258
00:29:39,280 --> 00:29:45,119
kind of action, what kind of speech and eventually\h
what kind of thought I was cultivating during the\h\h

259
00:29:45,120 --> 00:29:51,359
day is like basically to kind of cultivate a\h
sense of shame. Tcultivate the right sense\h\h

260
00:29:51,360 --> 00:29:58,639
of shame. And remorse doesn't happen automatically.\h
It's just kind of forgetting literally this\h\h

261
00:29:58,640 --> 00:30:04,159
very sense of shame, because if I was shameful\h
enough this action, I would have actually avoided.\h\h

262
00:30:04,160 --> 00:30:11,919
And basically now I'm ashamed of
lacking enough shame, so I just act out\h\h

263
00:30:11,920 --> 00:30:17,679
of shamelessness really, and I just act out in\h
those ways and that's why it feels like difficult,\h\h

264
00:30:17,680 --> 00:30:22,479
because now I have to kind of recollect and feel\h
that shame, it has to be felt like and that's\h\h

265
00:30:22,480 --> 00:30:28,319
painful and it has to be felt without trying\h
to get rid of it or, and certainly not justifying\h\h

266
00:30:28,320 --> 00:30:35,119
it. Just kind of feeling it and make the\h
determination, say: "Okay, next time I won't do it",\h\h

267
00:30:35,120 --> 00:30:40,959
but now I did so I have to endure the things and I\h
think that's why those practicse are\h\h

268
00:30:40,960 --> 00:30:46,879
so beneficial, but at the same time kind of\h
hard, because you have to endure the\h\h

269
00:30:46,880 --> 00:30:52,799
things over and over again and this\h
attitude of justifying the mind, the\h\h

270
00:30:52,800 --> 00:30:58,479
mind trying to find justification and things like\h
that; it seems much more easy, but that's the very\h\h

271
00:30:58,480 --> 00:31:04,719
reason why, because I was justifying myself. That's\h
the very reason why I give into those actions that\h\h

272
00:31:04,720 --> 00:31:09,359
now I feel shameful of. So I think it kind\h
of also follows like what you were saying before,

273
00:31:09,360 --> 00:31:13,919
it's black and white. You cannot do both at the\h
same time. Whether I cultivate the sense\h\h

274
00:31:13,920 --> 00:31:20,319
of shame or I just: "That's okay, that's all right."\h
You either cover it; you're either covering it\h\h

275
00:31:20,320 --> 00:31:27,039
or you are learning to see... And that is also\h
why it is for a noble disciple one\h\h

276
00:31:27,040 --> 00:31:31,439
of the first faculties that the Buddha often\h
talks about: shame, so the right\h\h

277
00:31:31,440 --> 00:31:37,919
shame, based on the correct understanding, and now\h
the fear of wrongdoing; right fear of wrongdoing,\h\h

278
00:31:37,920 --> 00:31:47,039
like not wanting to now start agitating that mind\h
that you basically were taming. So yes, that's\h\h

279
00:31:47,040 --> 00:31:53,679
good. And and it even says in the context of monks\h
and their precepts, even if you

280
00:31:53,680 --> 00:32:01,999
break one of those precepts - I mean, it's better to\h
not do do that, but if you, let's\h\h

281
00:32:02,000 --> 00:32:07,759
say, break one of those precepts - then to correct them, to realize that you made a\h\h

282
00:32:07,760 --> 00:32:14,319
mistake, confess it, correct it and then determine\h
to not do it again. And then the Buddha says "Yes,\h\h

283
00:32:14,320 --> 00:32:20,879
this is how there's progress in dhamma", basically\h
just what you described. Now, that whole same\h\h

284
00:32:20,880 --> 00:32:25,599
principle applies even if you do not break any\h
of the precepts that have been formulated, but as\h\h

285
00:32:25,600 --> 00:32:31,439
we said, at some point you want to keep that same\h
attitude until even on the level of the thoughts\h\h

286
00:32:31,440 --> 00:32:38,399
that you welcome and delight in, or the things that\h
you anticipate, at some point it will come to that.\h\h

287
00:32:38,400 --> 00:32:44,399
And then you say: "Wait, I don't want to delight\h
in it, I don't want to welcome it" and yes, then\h\h

288
00:32:44,400 --> 00:32:50,079
there are certain discourses that deal with how\h
to remove obstructing thoughts and that's going\h\h

289
00:32:50,080 --> 00:32:55,359
to be a whole separate discussion, because\h
thoughts can arise on their own, so you have to\h\h

290
00:32:55,360 --> 00:32:59,279
endure, but at some point you will also recognize:\h
"Wait, I'm actually now feeding those thoughts" and\h\h

291
00:32:59,280 --> 00:33:07,599
that you should stop. And we can call it "thought",\h
but there's further development\h\h

292
00:33:07,600 --> 00:33:14,639
there. But we can discuss that perhaps on another\h
occasion. But all of that will depend on\h\h

293
00:33:14,640 --> 00:33:19,839
understanding what we're saying now, because\h
it's essentially for the mind not different than\h\h

294
00:33:19,840 --> 00:33:25,070
when you started with the precepts for the body\h
and speech, which is why it's a gradual training.